.TH HAUSLICHT\-CTL 1 "2026-10-22" "Haus.Licht 0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hauslicht\-ctl \- send one raw command to the hauslicht daemon and print its JSON reply
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hauslicht\-ctl
[\fB@\fR\fIN\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBhauslicht\-ctl\fR is the small client the Plasma widget uses. It sends one
request to the running daemon (\fBhauslicht sync\fR) over its Unix socket and
prints \fBresults[0]\fR of the reply as one JSON line. It needs neither the
virtual environment nor Bluetooth libraries. For readable output use
\fBhauslicht\fR(1), which offers the same commands.
.PP
Each request is one JSON line,
\fB{"cmd": "set", "arg": "range_hi=150", "args": ["range_hi=150"], "which": null}\fR,
answered by \fB{"results": [...]}\fR or \fB{"error": "message"}\fR.
Error messages are meant for the user (German when the daemon runs in a German locale).
.PP
A command may start with \fB@\fR\fIN\fR: it then acts in widget \fIN\fR's
context (its bulb list, selection, profile and clap mode); without it, widget 1.
\(lqThe selection\(rq below is the widget's: \fBall\fR (the used bulbs of the
widget, profile \fBdefault\fR) or one bulb.
.PP
Most commands reply with the \fBget\fR object; the exceptions are noted.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS State
.TP
\fBget\fR
The state of the widget: selection, power, profile values, settings, every bulb
(\fBbulbs_list\fR), backends. The fields are described in docs/ctl\-protocol.md.
.TP
\fBwatch\fR
Keep the connection open: a snapshot (the \fBget\fR object plus each bulb's
intended state), then one JSON line per event (\fBintended\fR, \fBflick\fR,
\fBdropout\fR, \fBreturned\fR, \fBconnected\fR, \fBdisconnected\fR, \fBclap\fR,
\fBaudio\fR, \fBvideo\fR, \fBidentify\fR, \fBtest\fR) until interrupted.
.TP
\fBstatus\fR
Replies \fB[{bulb, on, percent}]\fR as the devices report it.
.SS Switch and dim
.TP
\fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR | \fBtoggle\fR
Power of the selection (toggle: any on, then all off, else all on).
.TP
\fBpause\fR | \fBresume\fR
Stop or restart following for the selection.
.TP
\fBbulb\fR \fIN\fR
The widget's brightness slider, 0\-100: the profile's \fBlevel\fR while the
screen is ignored, otherwise a manual level until \fBresume\fR.
.TP
\fBdim\fR \fIN\fR
A manual level sent as it is (0 is off); replies \fB[{bulb, percent}]\fR.
.TP
\fBslider\fR \fIN\fR
The keyboard slider, 0\-100.
.TP
\fBnudge\fR \fI\(+-N\fR
Move the slider by \fIN\fR; in mode \fBoff\fR the brightness of the selection.
.TP
\fBselect\fR \fIID\fR | \fBall\fR
What the slider, nudge, profile keys and on/off act on.
.TP
\fBraw\fR \fIHEX\fR[,\fIHEX\fR...]
Mesh only: send access payloads, reply \fB[{bulb, sent, replies}]\fR.
.SS Settings
.TP
\fBset\fR \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR
Globals: \fBmode\fR (independent minimum maximum coupling off), \fBlid\fR
(close open both never), \fBmonitor_power\fR (on off), \fBflick\fR (off on fast
exclusive), \fBexclusive_adapter\fR (hciN, an address, auto), \fBwidgets\fR
(1\-8), \fBautoconnect\fR, \fBclap\fR (the widget's mode: off clap snap both),
\fBclap_source\fR, \fBclap_sensitivity\fR (0\-100), \fBaudio_source\fR,
\fBaudio_color\fR, \fBfollow_rate\fR (1\-3), \fBvideo_max\fR (0\-100),
\fBvideo_smooth\fR (0 0.2 0.5 1 2 5), \fBnotify\fR.
Keys of the selection's profile: \fBtrack\fR (always night day dark light
never), \fBoff_at_zero\fR, \fBrange_lo\fR, \fBrange_hi\fR (\-100 to 200, never
crossing), \fBcurve\fR (\-100 to 100), \fBlevel\fR (0\-100), \fBhue\fR (0\-360),
\fBsat\fR (0\-100), \fBfollow_ct\fR (on off), \fBshift\fR (0\-200).
.TP
\fBmode\fR | \fBtrack\fR | \fBoff\-at\-zero\fR | \fBlid\fR | \fBmonitor\-power\fR | \fBflick\fR | \fBautoconnect\fR | \fBclap\fR | \fBclap\-source\fR | \fBclap\-sensitivity\fR [\fIVALUE\fR]
The same as \fBset\fR, the value optional.
.SS Profiles
.TP
\fBprofile save\fR \fINAME\fR
Copy the selection's profile values into \fINAME\fR.
.TP
\fBprofile apply\fR \fINAME\fR
One bulb selected: it uses \fINAME\fR. \fBall\fR: \fINAME\fR's values go into
\fBdefault\fR, and every bulb of the widget uses \fBdefault\fR.
.TP
\fBprofile rename\fR \fIOLD NEW\fR | \fBprofile delete\fR \fINAME\fR
Bulbs follow a rename; on delete they fall back to \fBdefault\fR, which can be
neither renamed nor deleted. \fBpreset\fR is an alias of \fBprofile\fR.
.SS Bulbs and widgets
.TP
\fBbulb\-set\fR \fIID\fR \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR ...
\fBname=\fR, \fBprofile=\fR, \fBuse=\fRon|off, \fBicon_on=\fR, \fBicon_off=\fR,
\fBicon_disconnected=\fR (an icon name or an absolute path; empty: none).
\fBuse=off\fR makes the bulb idle: OnPowerUp back to on, then nothing else is
sent to it.
.TP
\fBwidget\-bulbs\fR [\fIID\fR,\fIID\fR...]
The widget's bulbs (none: empty), stored in list order.
.TP
\fBorder\fR \fIID ID\fR ...
The list order: every bulb id once.
.TP
\fBidentify\fR \fIID\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]
Blink the bulb slowly to find it, until \fBoff\fR, a flick or 120 s.
.TP
\fBtest\fR \fIID\fR [\fBstop\fR]
About 25 s: brightest, a fade, colours, then on/off fast to slow; \fBtesting\fR
in \fBget\fR reports the step and the fastest on/off rate. Flashes quickly: not
for people sensitive to flashing light.
.SS Add and remove bulbs
.TP
\fBdiscover\fR [\fIKIND\fR | \fBbluetooth\fR]
Replies \fB{discovered: [{kind, address, name, config, known}], errors, access}\fR
after about 5 s.
.TP
\fBadd\fR \fIKIND\fR \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR ...
Add a bulb; the keys are the backend's \fBfields\fR (see \fBbackends\fR),
\fBname=\fR names it. Replies the \fBget\fR object plus \fB"added": ID\fR.
.TP
\fBremove\fR \fIID\fR
Stop controlling the bulb; an autoconnected one goes on the ignore list.
.TP
\fBunignore\fR \fIID\fR | \fIADDRESS\fR
Take a device off \fBautoconnect_ignore\fR.
.TP
\fBbackends\fR
Replies \fB{backends: [{kind, title, available, missing, fields}]}\fR.
.SS Wall switch and Bluetooth
.TP
\fBadapters\fR
Replies the Bluetooth adapters flick mode \fBexclusive\fR can take, the selected
and the chosen one.
.TP
\fBsetup\-root\fR
Run \fBpkexec install/setup\-root.sh\fR in the background (a graphical prompt);
\fBsetup.running\fR in \fBget\fR tells when it is done.
.TP
\fBsetup\-status\fR
The \fBget\fR object with the setup status read anew.
.SS Clap and follow audio
.TP
\fBclap\-sources\fR
Replies \fB{sources: [{name, description, default, muted}], selected}\fR: the microphones.
.TP
\fBclap\-meter\fR
Replies the test meter: level, noise floor, the last judged sound and the last double.
.TP
\fBclap\-hits\fR
Replies the last 30 hits with the gate's numbers, scores and verdict.
.TP
\fBaudio\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fBtoggle\fR
The selection follows what the computer plays: loudness to brightness, pitch to colour.
.TP
\fBaudio\-source\fR | \fBaudio\-color\fR | \fBaudio\-rate\fR | \fBfollow\-rate\fR [\fIVALUE\fR]
The globals \fBaudio_source\fR, \fBaudio_color\fR and \fBfollow_rate\fR
(\fBaudio\-rate\fR is its old name).
.TP
\fBaudio\-sources\fR
Replies every source, sink monitors first, and the selected one.
.TP
\fBaudio\-status\fR
Replies \fBget\fR's \fBaudio_status\fR alone.
.SS Follow video
.TP
\fBvideo\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fBtoggle\fR
The selection follows its region of the screen.
.TP
\fBvideo\-region\fR \fIID REGION\fR
\fBwhole\fR, \fBleft\fR, \fBright\fR, \fBtop\fR, \fBbottom\fR, \fBtop\-left\fR,
\fBtop\-right\fR, \fBbottom\-left\fR or \fBbottom\-right\fR.
.TP
\fBvideo\-max\fR | \fBvideo\-smooth\fR [\fIVALUE\fR]
The brightness of the brightest screen; the time averaging in seconds.
.TP
\fBvideo\-forget\fR
Forget the screen permission; the next start asks again.
.TP
\fBvideo\-status\fR
Replies \fBget\fR's \fBvideo_status\fR alone.
.SS Notification blinks
.TP
\fBnotify\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]
The global switch (default off).
.TP
\fBnotify\-rules\fR
Replies \fB{profile, rules}\fR: the rules of the selection's profile.
.TP
\fBnotify\-rules\-set\fR \fIJSON\fR
Replace that profile's rule list, validated as a whole. A rule:
\fB{name, app, title, body, pattern, count, intensity, color, bulbs, dark}\fR.
.TP
\fBnotify\-test\fR \fIRULE\fR
Run the rule's pattern now; \fBnotify_test\fR lists the bulbs that blink.
.TP
\fBnotify\-status\fR
Replies \fBget\fR's \fBnotify_status\fR alone.
.SS Test instance only
.TP
\fBmock\-flick\fR \fIID\fR | \fBmock\-return\fR \fIID GAP\fR | \fBmock\-offline\fR \fIID\fR
Simulate a wall-switch flick, a return after \fIGAP\fR seconds, or an outage
of a mock bulb.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBHAUSLICHT_SOCK\fR
Another daemon's socket (a test instance).
.TP
\fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR
The socket is \fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hauslicht.sock\fR unless \fBHAUSLICHT_SOCK\fR is set.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hauslicht.sock\fR
The daemon's socket.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
The daemon answered; the reply is printed.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The daemon is not reachable or refused the command; \fB{"error": ...}\fR is printed.
.TP
\fB2\fR
No command given; the usage is printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
hauslicht\-ctl get
hauslicht\-ctl set range_hi=150
hauslicht\-ctl @2 select mesh:0003
hauslicht\-ctl bulb\-set mesh:0002 name=Desk use=on
hauslicht\-ctl watch
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBhauslicht\fR(1); docs/ctl\-protocol.md in the checkout: every command and every
field of \fBget\fR.
